{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_37","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":25000,"raw":false,"start_index":0,"url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2024/country-chapters/united-states"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2024/country-chapters/united-states:\nRacism, anti-immigrant sentiments, and threats to democracy remained pressing human rights problems in the United States in 2023. The national poverty rate rose dramatically following the choice to cease a pandemic-period child tax credit. Economic inequality also rose, and the racial wealth gap remained high. The incarceration rate also increased, despite the US already having one of the highest rates in the world, with Black people vastly overrepresented in prisons and jails.\n\nIn its foreign policy, the US held human rights abusers accountable through targeted sanctions and provided new support to international justice mechanisms such as the investigation of the International Criminal Court (ICC) into the conflict in Ukraine. The US also undermined its stated commitment to human rights by providing military assistance to human rights-abusing states.\n\nThe administration of US President Joe Biden banned the government’s use of abusive commercial spyware and issued some policies aimed at improving racial equity in efforts to tackle climate change at home and abroad. The US Supreme Court issued decisions reinforcing laws aimed at protecting the right to vote.\n\nHowever, states enacted an increasing number of laws that restrict access to reproductive care, including abortion, and the rights of lesbian, gay, bisexual, and transgender (LGBT) people. The federal government has not taken sufficient steps necessary to limit global warming, even though the US is among the world’s top greenhouse gas emitters. State and federal authorities continued to pursue policies aimed at deterring people from seeking asylum in the US, in flagrant violation of international human rights law, pushing them to take more dangerous routes.\n\nA federal task force [documented](https://apnews.com/article/election-workers-threats-justice-department-trump-f17df44d63156a28eaa0862b0aa08f0b) harassment and threats against election officials, revealing the need to better protect election administrators from intimidation and to address the spread of misinformation and disinformation.\n\n## Racial Justice\n\nThe racial wealth gap [remained stark](https://www.stlouisfed.org/institute-for-economic-equity/the-state-of-us-wealth-inequality#:~:text=White%20households%20continue%20to%20own,in%20the%20U.S.%20might%20predict.), with Black families having 24 cents and Hispanic families having 23 cents for every US$1 of white family wealth, and has changed very little over the last 50 years. [Numerous](https://ir.citi.com/NvIUklHPilz14Hwd3oxqZBLMn1_XPqo5FrxsZD0x6hhil84ZxaxEuJUWmak51UHvYk75VKeHCMI%3D) [studies](https://www.brookings.edu/articles/why-we-need-reparations-for-black-americans/) have shown that drastic economic intervention, including reparations in a variety of forms, is needed to address this gap as well as [continuing racial disparities](https://www.hrw.org/news/2021/02/17/hr-40-exploring-path-reparative-justice-america) in access to adequate health, nutrition, education, employment, and housing, among other things.\n\nIn May, US Representative Cori Bush introduced a [resolution](https://bush.house.gov/media/press-releases/congresswoman-bush-introduces-legislation-urging-federal-reparations#:~:text=Washington%20D.C.%20(May%2018%2C%202023,forms%20those%20reparations%20should%20take.) urging the federal government to provide reparations for enslavement and its legacies and to support existing proposals such as H.R. 40. H.R. 40 is a House of Representatives bill that proposes establishing a federal commission to study and make recommendations on reparations, which has been introduced every congressional session since 1989 but has yet to pass. Congressional leadership did not bring either the new resolution or the House bill to the floor for a vote.\n\nWhile federal [efforts](https://www.congress.gov/bill/118th-congress/house-resolution/414/actions?s=4&r=1&q=%7B%22search%22%3A%22study+reparations%22%7D) [stalled](https://www.congress.gov/bill/117th-congress/house-bill/40/all-actions-without-amendments?q=%7B%22search%22%3A%5B%22study+reparations%22%5D%7D&s=1&r=2), states made progress on reparations. In May, a California reparations task force, created by 2020 legislation, submitted [reparatory proposals](https://www.cnn.com/2023/05/10/us/california-reparations-task-force-newsom-reaj/index.html) to the state governor for consideration. Also in May, [Washington state](https://www.axios.com/2023/05/09/washington-state-reparations-real-estate-covenants) enacted a law to create a downpayment assistance program for people directly affected by past racist housing covenants. In June, the [New York](https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/nation/2023/06/09/new-york-reparations-bill/70305589007/) state legislature passed a bill to study the economic impacts of enslavement and the government’s role in supporting that institution, which the governor had not signed at time of writing.\n\nIn July, a lawsuit seeking [reparations](https://www.washingtonpost.com/nation/2023/07/09/tulsa-race-riot-lawsuit-reparations-dismissed/) for the last three known survivors of the 1921 Tulsa Race Massacre was [dismissed](https://www.nbcnews.com/news/nbcblk/tulsa-massacre-survivors-appeal-dismissed-case-oklahoma-supreme-court-rcna98575) by an Oklahoma judge. The survivors appealed, and in August, the Oklahoma Supreme Court [agreed to hear the case](https://www.cnn.com/2023/08/15/us/oklahoma-supreme-court-to-hear-tulsa-race-massacre-appeal-reaj/index.html). In October, Hughes Van Ellis, [who at 102 was the youngest of the three living survivors, died](https://www.abc27.com/news/us-world/ap-hughes-van-ellis-youngest-known-survivor-of-tulsa-race-massacre-dies-at-102/).\n\n## Economic Justice\n\nAfter two years of historic declines in poverty due to [expanded social protection](https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/03/07/turning-covids-economic-crisis-opportunity) in response to Covid-19, the US Supplemental Poverty Measure, which incorporates the influence of government assistance and geographical cost of living differences, [rose dramatically](https://www.nytimes.com/2023/09/12/business/economy/income-poverty-health-insurance.html?campaign_id=60&emc=edit_na_20230912&instance_id=0&nl=breaking-news&ref=cta&regi_id=3918362&segment_id=144531&user_id=b74a0bbbeb4387fe5d81e7f0e7010cad), jumping to 12.4 percent in 2022 from 7.8 percent in 2021. Income inequality in the US is [very high](https://wir2022.wid.world/) compared to [other wealthy countries](https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/SI.POV.GINI?locations=OE&most_recent_value_desc=false), with the top 10 percent of earners capturing nearly half of all income and the bottom 50 percent getting just 13 percent.\n\nAfter taxes and government transfers are considered, the Gini index, a statistical measure of income inequality, for the US has [increased](https://www.census.gov/newsroom/press-releases/2023/income-poverty-health-insurance-coverage.html) by 3.2 percent since 2021. Wealth inequality is [similarly stark](https://wir2022.wid.world/www-site/uploads/2023/03/D_FINAL_WIL_RIM_RAPPORT_2303.pdf), with the poorest 50 percent of the US population owning only just 1.5 percent of the country’s private wealth.\n\n## Criminal Justice\n\nThe US has one of the [highest incarceration rates](https://www.prisonstudies.org/highest-to-lowest/prison_population_rate?field_region_taxonomy_tid=All) in the world, with roughly [2 million](https://www.vera.org/ending-mass-incarceration) people held in state and [federal jails, prisons, and immigration detention facilities](https://www.prisonpolicy.org/reports/pie2023.html) on any given day, and millions more on parole and probation. Despite some [reductions in incarceration rates for Black people](https://www.pewresearch.org/fact-tank/2020/05/06/share-of-black-white-hispanic-americans-in-prison-2018-vs-2006/), they remain [vastly overrepresented](http://cdn.cnn.com/cnn/2021/images/10/13/the-color-of-justice-racial-and-ethnic-disparity-in-state-prisons.pdf) in jails and prisons.\n\nIn 2021, the most recent year for which such data is available, the rate at which people were incarcerated in jails and prisons nationwide[increased for the first time](https://bjs.ojp.gov/document/cpus21st.pdf) since 2005, although it remained below pre-pandemic levels.\n\nWidespread calls [to reduce overreliance on policing](https://static1.squarespace.com/static/5ee39ec764dbd7179cf1243c/t/60806839979abc1b93aa8695/1619028044655/%23DefundThePolice%2BUpdate.pdf) and [address societal problems](https://www.hrw.org/news/2020/08/12/us-14-recommendations-fundamental-police-reform) instead with investments in housing, health care, and education were largely drowned out [by calls for more police funding](https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/05/17/groups-express-concerns-over-policing-bills) and the [rollback of police reforms](https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/03/24/us-dont-roll-back-new-york-pretrial-reforms). The latter calls were driven by [persistent misinformation](https://www.fwd.us/news/new-report-highlights-how-misleading-media-coverage-has-ignored-clear-data-showing-success-of-new-yorks-bail-reform-laws/) and [misleading narratives](https://equalityalec.substack.com/p/how-to-tell-a-lie-with-the-truth?utm_source=profile&utm_medium=reader2) about [rising crime rates](https://fair.org/home/nyt-twists-stats-to-insist-we-need-more-policing/) and decreased public safety, often generated and manipulated by law enforcement, despite substantial evidence of the success of reforms and community investments.\n\nLocal governments criminalized unhoused communities [across](https://nlihc.org/sites/default/files/AG-2021/06-08_Criminalization-of-Homelessness.pdf#:~:text=From%202006-2019%2C%20the%20National%20Homelessness%20Law%20Center%20has,medicines%2C%20and%20what%20little%20shelter%20the%20residents%20have.) the country and [expanded forced or coerced treatment](https://theappeal.org/assisted-outpatient-treatment-criminalization-mental-illnes/) to [address](https://www.hrw.org/news/2023/08/07/human-rights-watchs-opposition-sb-43) people [living on the streets](https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/12/02/new-york-mayor-targets-unhoused-communities).\n\nMost US police departments refuse to report data on their use of force, necessitating nongovernmental data collection and analysis. As of September 28, police had killed over [800 people](https://mappingpoliceviolence.org/) in 2023, similar to numbers in prior years. On a per capita basis, police kill Black people at almost three times the rate they kill white people.\n\n## Youth Justice\n\nChildren [continue to be tried as adults](https://www.hrw.org/news/2023/09/07/no-us-state-meets-child-rights-standards) in all 50 states, despite [international standards](https://www.ohchr.org/en/instruments-mechanisms/instruments/united-nations-standard-minimum-rules-administration-juvenile) repudiating the practice. Those transferred to the adult system are [disproportionately youth of color](https://www.socialworkers.org/News/News-Releases/ID/1679/Report-Black-youth-continue-to-be-sent-to-adult-courts-and-jails-at-alarmingly-high-rates), with racial and ethnic disparities persisting at almost [every point of contact](https://www.sentencingproject.org/app/uploads/2023/05/Youth-Justice-by-the-Numbers.pdf) with the justice system, including arrests, pre-disposition detention, and post-adjudication incarceration.\n\nIn 2023, [three states](https://www.hrw.org/news/2023/09/07/no-us-state-meets-child-rights-standards) took steps toward eliminating the sentence of life without parole (LWOP) for children. In total, [33 states and](https://cfsy.org/) Washington, DC, have now banned it or have no one serving such a sentence. Despite this progress, the US remains the only country in the world to sentence children convicted of crimes to die in prison.\n\n## Child Labor\n\nIn February, the US Department of Labor reported a [sharp increase](https://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/osec/osec20230227) in child labor violations, and investigations carried out by [media outlets](https://www.nytimes.com/2023/02/25/us/unaccompanied-migrant-child-workers-exploitation.html) showed children, often unaccompanied migrants, working in dangerous and exploitative conditions, sacrificing their health, safety, and education. Some states moved to [roll back child labor protections](https://www.epi.org/publication/child-labor-laws-under-attack/). Longstanding exemptions in US labor laws allow children as young as 12 to work legally in [agriculture](https://www.newsweek.com/us-should-end-child-labor-agriculture-opinion-1797673), the deadliest sector for child workers.\n\n## Drug Policy\n\nOverdose deaths continued to rise, reaching another record level. They first [surpassed 100,000 in the 12-month period ending April 2021](https://www.nbcnews.com/health/health-news/fentanyl-stimulants-drives-fourth-wave-overdose-epidemic-us-rcna104953) and increased to [111,355](https://www.cnn.com/2023/09/13/health/overdose-deaths-record-april-2023/index.html#:~:text=There%20were%20111%2C355%20overdose%20deaths,everyday%20life%2C%E2%80%9D%20said%20Dr.) in the 12-month period ending April 2023. Racial disparities in overdose deaths [also continued to widen](https://www.bostonglobe.com/2023/03/02/opinion/black-opioid-crisis-reveals-that-barriers-treatment-must-come-down/), with the [rate of Black deaths](https://www.uclahealth.org/news/racial-disparities-us-drug-overdose-fatalities-increased) exceeding those of white ones, due in part to [racial bias in policies](https://www.vera.org/publications/overdose-deaths-and-jail-incarceration/national-trends-and-racial-disparities) and [access to treatment](https://store.samhsa.gov/sites/default/files/pep20-05-02-001.pdf).\n\nFederal and state authorities continue to rely significantly on [criminalization](https://www.hrw.org/news/2023/09/26/rights-groups-urge-congress-support-test-act) to address harmful drug use, even though harm reduction strategies that offer health-centered care and access to voluntary treatment [have proven](https://www.hrw.org/news/2021/12/10/misguided-us-approach-drug-overdose-deaths) [more effective](https://www.hrw.org/news/2016/10/12/us-disastrous-toll-criminalizing-drug-use).\n\nIn 2022, the Biden administration became [the first US administration](https://www.courthousenews.com/biden-quietly-making-a-radical-shift-in-opioid-policy/) to invest in harm reduction, but stronger and more robust investments in health-centered approaches are needed. In March, the US Food and Drug Administration [approved naloxone, the first over-the-counter drug](https://www.npr.org/2023/03/29/1166750095/narcan-fda-approval-naloxone-over-the-counter-otc) used to reverse opioid overdose.\n\n## Immigrants’ Rights and Border Communities\n\nThe 2020 [Title 42](https://www.hrw.org/news/2021/04/08/qa-us-title-42-policy-expel-migrants-border) summary expulsions policy expired in May but was replaced by a [new labyrinthine asylum rule](https://www.hrw.org/news/2023/05/11/us-biden-asylum-ban-endangers-lives-border). Under Title 42, justified as an emergency measure to combat the Covid-19 pandemic, the Biden administration summarily[expelled people 2.8 million times](https://www.wola.org/2023/05/weekly-u-s-mexico-border-update-title-42-ends/#:~:text=Over%20its%2038%20months%20of,migrants%20over%202.8%20million%20times.) without [screening them for asylum](https://www.wola.org/2023/05/weekly-u-s-mexico-border-update-title-42-ends/#:~:text=Over%20its%2038%20months%20of,migrants%20over%202.8%20million%20times.).\n\nUnder the new asylum rule, many asylum seekers face expedited removals, arbitrary detention, prosecution, and a five-year ban on returning to the US unless they make an appointment at select US land border ports of entry using “CBP One” prior to crossing the border. CBP One is [a difficult-to-access phone app](https://www.hrw.org/news/2023/05/11/us-biden-asylum-ban-endangers-lives-border) that often fails to recognize faces with darker skin tones. This process can take several months and exposes asylum seekers to [systematic targeting by cartels and Mexican government officials](https://www.hrw.org/news/2020/06/02/dhs-oig-formal-complaint-regarding-remain-mexico#:~:text=Human%20Rights%20Watch%20submits%20this,accountable%20for%20knowingly%20subjecting%20asylum) for kidnapping, extortion, sexual assault, and other harms.\n\nAs a candidate, [Biden pledged to end private immigration detention](https://www.reuters.com/world/us/biden-vowed-reform-immigration-detention-instead-private-prisons-benefited-2023-08-07/), but as of July, [90 percent of the 30,000 non-citizens who are in detention on average each day](https://www.aclu.org/news/immigrants-rights/unchecked-growth-private-prison-corporations-and-immigration-detention-three-years-into-the-biden-administration) in the US were held in private facilities.\n\nIn June, Texas Governor Greg Abbott ramped up his already ruthless border approach by installing razor wire and buoys with circular saws in or near the Rio Grande. Under Operation Lone Star, [high-speed vehicle pursuits](https://www.hrw.org/report/2023/11/27/so-much-blood-ground/dangerous-and-deadly-vehicle-pursuits-under-texas-operation) and the accidents they cause in communities throughout south Texas [are a threat to public safety](https://www.hrw.org/news/2023/07/26/statement-human-rights-watch-human-cost-harsh-us-immigration-deterrence-policies#:~:text=Human%20Rights%20Watch%20and%20other,by%20the%20US%20government%20that), with harmful consequences for migrant passengers and Texas residents alike. Human Rights Watch found that at least 74 people have been killed and 189 injured since the policy began in March 2021.\n\n## Democracy\n\nThe battle to defend multiracial democracy in the US continued. In June, the US Supreme Court, in Allen v. Milligan, [struck down](https://www.nytimes.com/2023/06/08/us/supreme-court-voting-rights-act-alabama.html) Alabama’s gerrymandered congressional maps, reaffirming that racial discrimination in voting laws, maps, and practices is illegal. Also in June, the US Supreme Court, in [Moore v. Harper](https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/12/05/human-rights-watch-amicus-brief-moore-v-harper), upheld the right of people in the US to seek a remedy for voting rights violations in state courts. However, at least 14 states [passed laws](https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/research-reports/voting-laws-roundup-october-2023) in 2023 that make it more difficult to vote.\n\nState-level lawmakers continued to undermine democracy by [banning books](https://pen.org/report/banned-in-the-usa-state-laws-supercharge-book-suppression-in-schools/) and [passing laws](https://www.commondreams.org/news/gop-educational-gag-orders-2023) that restrict truthful classroom discussions of race, history, sexual orientation, and gender. This censorship has the potential to undermine civic participation by erasing the galvanizing stories of ordinary citizens who organized to promote human rights.\n\nThe movement for universal suffrage earned victories. [New Mexico](https://www.sentencingproject.org/press-releases/the-sentencing-project-applauds-voting-rights-reform-in-new-mexico/) and [Minnesota](https://www.npr.org/2023/08/04/1192295228/mississippi-felon-voting-law-blocked) passed laws that allow individuals to vote upon release from prison. A federal court [overturned](https://www.npr.org/2023/08/04/1192295228/mississippi-felon-voting-law-blocked) Mississippi’s lifetime voting ban for individuals convicted of some felony offenses, calling the practice cruel and unusual.\n\nFor the first time in US history, a former president faced significant sanctions, including criminal and civil charges, in part for his efforts to overturn the 2020 elections, a serious infringement of the right to vote. New [studies](https://apnews.com/article/election-workers-threats-justice-department-trump-f17df44d63156a28eaa0862b0aa08f0b) by the federal government and [civil society](https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/policy-solutions/securing-2024-election) groups, revealed vulnerabilities in US democracy, including the need to better protect elections administrators from threats and [intimidation](https://www.reuters.com/investigates/section/campaign-of-fear/), which continued to increase. Since 2020, when the US Department of Justice opened a task force into the matter, [14 investigations into](https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-departments-election-threats-task-force-secures-ninth-conviction) attempts to threaten election workers have been initiated, 9 of which had resulted in findings of wrongdoing at time of writing. The year also revealed the need to better address the spread of [misinformation and disinformation](https://www.politico.com/news/2023/07/29/election-disinformation-campaigns-targeted-voters-of-color-in-2020-experts-expect-2024-to-be-worse-00108866) through social media platforms.\n\n## Climate Change Policy and Impacts\n\nThe US remains the world’s [largest oil and gas producer](https://priceofoil.org/content/uploads/2023/09/OCI-Planet-Wreckers-Report.pdf) and historically is the country that has [most contributed](https://www.carbonbrief.org/analysis-which-countries-are-historically-responsible-for-climate-change) to the climate crisis. It also remains among the world’s top greenhouse gas emitters.\n\nThe Biden administration [issued an executive order](https://www.hrw.org/news/2023/04/24/white-house-issues-significant-environmental-justice-order-earth-day) that directs all federal agencies to incorporate the pursuit of environmental justice into their missions and announced the creation of a [new policy committee](https://www.whitehouse.gov/briefing-room/statements-releases/2023/04/21/fact-sheet-president-biden-signs-executive-order-to-revitalize-our-nations-commitment-to-environmental-justice-for-all/) to coordinate efforts to prioritize public health, economic development, and equity in tackling the global [plastics](https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/11/23/plastics-and-human-rights-questions-and-answers) pollution crisis. Virtually all plastics are derived from fossil fuels.\n\nHowever, the US is also on track to be responsible for the [world’s largest expansion](https://priceofoil.org/content/uploads/2023/09/OCI-Planet-Wreckers-Report.pdf) in oil and gas extraction from 2023 to 2050. Fossil fuels are the single largest contributor to global warming and [can be linked to human rights harms](https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/11/16/governments-should-commit-fossil-fuel-phase-out-cop27). These impacts are [disproportionately borne](https://www.hrw.org/news/2023/03/23/black-residents-louisiana-file-landmark-lawsuit) by already marginalized communities—including Black, Indigenous, and other people of color and low-income communities—and perpetuate systemic racism.\n\n## Women’s and Girls’ Rights\n\nThe June 2022 US Supreme Court decision in Dobbs v. Jackson Women’s Health Organization, overturning the constitutionally protected right to abortion, continued to have reverberating negative impacts on women, girls, and other people in the US who can become pregnant.\n\nAs of September 15, 22 states had banned abortion or restricted access to abortion at [earlier stages](https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2022/us/abortion-laws-roe-v-wade.html#:~:text=Twenty%2Dtwo%20states%20now%20ban,overturned%20the%20decision%20last%20year.) and [14 states](https://reproductiverights.org/maps/abortion-laws-by-state/) had enacted laws that criminalize healthcare providers who perform abortions. Some states also made it a crime for anyone, including healthcare providers, to assist pregnant people in obtaining an [abortion](https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2022/07/04/we-are-not-going-back-to-the-time-before-roe-we-are-going-somewhere-worse/amp). As a result, approximately 22 million women and girls of reproductive age, as well as other people who can become pregnant, now live in US states where abortion access is heavily restricted or inaccessible.\n\nIn August 2023, a federal appeals court, attempting to reconcile two conflicting lower court opinions [issued within minutes of each other](https://www.hrw.org/news/2023/04/10/conflicting-decisions-two-us-judges-rule-access-safe-abortion-drug)—one revoking the approval of mifepristone, a safe and effective drug used for medical abortions, and the other keeping it available—ruled that access to the drug should be limited in certain contexts: when mailed and prescribed via telemedicine. The US Department of Justice and the manufacturer [appealed the decision](https://www.axios.com/2023/09/08/abortion-pill-maker-supreme-court-review).\n\nRacial disparities in access to health care continue to leave millions of women of color at risk. Cervical cancer is a highly preventable and treatable disease, yet over 4,200 women die from it each year in the US, disproportionately [Black women in the South](https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/01/20/us-cervical-cancer-disproportionally-kills-black-women).\n\n## Disability Rights\n\n[P\n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 25000 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
