{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_43","type":"agent_message","text":"# Evidence-collection handoff: Joe Biden, United States, 2023\n\n**Job:** `dossier:2023-population-top20-gate5-v1:2023:USA:15811`  \n**Research period:** January–December 2023  \n**Ruler:** Joe Biden  \n**Access date:** 2026-07-17\n\n## Execution and persistence status\n\nThe supplied output directory was not writable in this read-only worker environment. I therefore could not persist:\n\n- `research-materials.md`\n- `research-ledger-manifest.json`\n\nThe complete substantive handoff appears below for parent persistence.\n\nWeb access was partially constrained:\n\n- Brave discovery calls returned HTTP 429 quota/rate-limit errors.\n- MiniMax Search worked and supplied discovery results.\n- Direct Fetch worked for several HTML pages.\n- Reuters prohibited direct autonomous fetching, so Reuters search-result snippets were retained only as discovery/context unless another opened source supported the same fact.\n- Some official PDFs were retrievable only as raw PDF bytes; without dependable page-text extraction, those were not treated as defensible locator-level evidence.\n- CBO and GAO blocked direct autonomous fetching. Their result snippets are useful discovery records but not full accepted evidence.\n\nNo additional local files were read.\n\n---\n\n# 1. Local-first baseline\n\nThe supplied local package contains **32 unique 2023 country-year facts**. None has warnings. They are high-confidence structured evidence for the fields measured, but they generally do **not** establish Biden’s personal conduct or causal contribution.\n\n## Chapter baseline\n\n| Chapter | Supplied local facts | Proper use | Main narrative/attribution gap |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1B | None | Explicit local-data gap; absence is not evidence of restraint or safety | Biden’s 2023 arms-control choices, nuclear posture, crisis conduct, safeguards, AI/cyber/space risk policy |\n| 2B | LF001–LF004 | Military-spending context only | Ukraine and Gaza diplomacy, escalation restraint, civilian protection, arms recipients, public justification, accountability |\n| 3B | LF005–LF010 | Country-level physical-integrity and repression baseline | Federal detention/asylum policy, police oversight, treatment of protest, vulnerable groups, ruler authority and remedies |\n| 4B | LF011–LF025 | Strong country-level democratic and rule-of-law baseline | Biden’s affirmative self-binding, appointments, media treatment, institutional restraint, digital-pressure controversy |\n| 5B | LF026–LF028 | Economic level/scale context | Biden-attributed policy, implementation, inflation/employment/fiscal management, distribution and trajectory |\n| 6B | None | Explicit local-data gap | Health, poverty, Medicaid, welfare access, vulnerable groups, crisis administration and durable institutions |\n| 7B | LF011, LF024, LF029–LF032 | Institutional corruption/accountability context only | Biden-specific candor, conflicts, family-business allegations, scrutiny and response |\n| 8B | LF011, LF024 | Inherited governance-capacity context only | Frozen program portfolio, operationalization, delivery, failures, adaptation, and attributable outcomes |\n\nThere are no `error_methodology_ids`; the empty 1B and 6B priors are ordinary local-data gaps, not extraction failures.\n\n---\n\n# 2. Global evidence register\n\nThese are stable provisional evidence items suitable for an append-only research notebook. Local priors are grouped where they share one materially coherent claim; the exact constituent fact IDs and locators remain listed.\n\n## Local structured evidence\n\n### LP-001 — US military expenditure in 2023\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `local-prior:2B.1|sipri_milex_USA_2023|military-expenditure-scale-and-share`\n- **Disposition:** context\n- **URLs/locators:** `local-prior:2B.1`\n- **Title/publisher/date:** SIPRI military-expenditure observations, 2023\n- **Claim:** The supplied package reports US military spending of approximately 943,032.8 in the source’s constant-USD scale, $2,674.69 per capita, 3.2937% of GDP, and 8.7481% of government spending.\n- **Exact fields:** LF001–LF004.\n- **Source type/confidence:** structured dataset; high.\n- **Limitations:** Spending magnitude does not establish aggression, restraint, necessity, procurement integrity, or Biden’s personal decisions.\n- **Period/attribution:** exact 2023 fit; country-year context, not automatic ruler attribution.\n- **Relevant lenses:** 2B.1, 2B.2, 2B.3, 2B.8, 2B.10; contextual 5B.3 and 8B.2.\n\n### LP-002 — Physical-integrity and repression baseline\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `local-prior:3B.1|USA_2023|physical-integrity-repression-baseline`\n- **Disposition:** context\n- **Locators:** `local-prior:3B.1`\n- **Title/publisher/date:** V-Dem and Political Terror Scale observations, 2023\n- **Claim:** V-Dem reports civil-society repression 1.915, extrajudicial-killing measure 2.857, physical-integrity liberties 0.949, and private civil liberties 0.901. Amnesty- and HRW-based PTS scores are both 3.0.\n- **Exact fields:** LF005–LF010.\n- **Source type/confidence:** structured datasets; high.\n- **Limitations:** Scale direction and substantive thresholds must be interpreted from dataset documentation; these observations do not identify incidents, federal versus subnational responsibility, Biden’s knowledge, or remedies.\n- **Period/attribution:** exact 2023 country-year baseline; ruler attribution unresolved.\n- **Relevant lenses:** 3B.1–3B.10 broadly, especially physical integrity, aligned actors, fear climate, and trajectory.\n\n### LP-003 — Democratic contestability and civil-liberties baseline\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `local-prior:4B.1|USA_2023|democratic-contestability-and-liberties`\n- **Disposition:** context\n- **Locators:** `local-prior:4B.1`\n- **Title/publisher/date:** Freedom House and V-Dem observations, 2023\n- **Claim:** Supplied indicators show Freedom House civil liberties and political rights of 2.0; V-Dem electoral democracy 0.857, association 0.931, expression 0.955, liberal democracy 0.789, multiparty institutions 0.775, regime type 3.0, and suffrage 1.0.\n- **Exact fields:** LF012–LF015, LF018–LF020, LF023, LF025.\n- **Source type/confidence:** structured datasets; high.\n- **Limitations:** Strong country institutions are inherited context and do not prove Biden’s affirmative restraint or strengthening.\n- **Period/attribution:** exact 2023 country-year baseline; limited personal attribution.\n- **Relevant lenses:** 4B.1–4B.3, 4B.5–4B.8, 4B.10; also contextual 3B.8.\n\n### LP-004 — Institutional constraints, accountability, and rule of law\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `local-prior:4B.1|USA_2023|constraints-accountability-rule-of-law`\n- **Disposition:** context\n- **Locators:** `local-prior:4B.1`\n- **Claim:** V-Dem reports accountability 1.641, judicial constraints 0.930, legislative constraints 0.933, and rule of law 0.972.\n- **Exact fields:** LF011, LF016, LF017, LF024.\n- **Source type/confidence:** structured dataset; high.\n- **Limitations:** These measure institutional conditions, not Biden’s personal integrity or effectiveness.\n- **Period/attribution:** exact 2023; inherited/state-capacity context.\n- **Relevant lenses:** 4B.2, 4B.4, 4B.8, 4B.10; context for 7B.6–7B.7 and 8B.3–8B.5.\n\n### LP-005 — Press-freedom baseline\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `local-prior:4B.1|USA_2023|press-freedom`\n- **Disposition:** context\n- **Locators:** `local-prior:4B.1`\n- **Claim:** RSF reports a 2023 press-freedom rank of 45 and score of 71.22.\n- **Exact fields:** LF021–LF022.\n- **Source type/confidence:** structured dataset; high.\n- **Limitations:** Does not specify federal executive conduct, local threats, media ownership, or individual incidents.\n- **Period/attribution:** exact 2023 country baseline.\n- **Relevant lenses:** 4B.3, 4B.6, 4B.9, 4B.10.\n\n### LP-006 — Economic scale and income baseline\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `local-prior:5B.1|USA_2023|gdp-population-baseline`\n- **Disposition:** context\n- **Locators:** `local-prior:5B.1`\n- **Claim:** World Bank WDI observations report real GDP per capita of approximately $81,032, total GDP of approximately $27.292 trillion, and population of 336.806 million.\n- **Exact fields:** LF026–LF028.\n- **Source type/confidence:** structured dataset; high.\n- **Limitations:** Level and scale do not establish distribution, household welfare, policy competence, or Biden’s contribution.\n- **Period/attribution:** exact 2023 country outcome; causal attribution unresolved.\n- **Relevant lenses:** 5B.1, 5B.3, 5B.6, 5B.8–5B.10; contextual 6B.10 and 8B.7.\n\n### LP-007 — Corruption baseline\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `local-prior:7B.1|USA_2023|corruption-context`\n- **Disposition:** context\n- **Locators:** `local-prior:7B.1`\n- **Claim:** Supplied observations report V-Dem political corruption 0.055, executive corruption 0.013, public-sector corruption 0.021, and Transparency International CPI 69.\n- **Exact fields:** LF029–LF032.\n- **Source type/confidence:** structured datasets; high.\n- **Limitations:** National corruption measures must not be personalized into claims about Biden’s honesty, family interests, or appointments.\n- **Period/attribution:** exact 2023 country institutional context; no direct ruler nexus.\n- **Relevant lenses:** contextual only for 7B.3–7B.7, 7B.9–7B.10.\n\n## Internet evidence\n\n### WEB-001 — Biden administration’s 2023 nuclear-risk strategy\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.armscontrol.org/events/2023-06/remarks-national-security-advisor-jake-sullivan-arms-control-association-aca-annual|sections-US-ready-engage-Russia-China-P5|arms-control-and-risk-reduction-program`\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence\n- **Title:** “Remarks by National Security Advisor Jake Sullivan for the Arms Control Association Annual Forum”\n- **Publisher/date:** Arms Control Association reproducing White House remarks, 2023-06-02\n- **Claim:** Sullivan, speaking f