Excluded from locator-qualified evidence count pending page/table retrieval.\n\n### WEB-018 — Formalized House impeachment inquiry, allegations unresolved in 2023\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.reuters.com/world/us/us-house-republicans-ready-vote-formalize-biden-impeachment-inquiry-2023-12-13/|search-result-summary|House-formalized-impeachment-inquiry`\n- **Disposition:** context\n- **Title:** “US House Republicans ready vote to formalize Biden impeachment inquiry”\n- **Publisher/date:** Reuters, 2023-12-13\n- **Claim:** The Republican-controlled House formally authorized an inquiry focused on allegations involving Hunter Biden’s business dealings; contemporaneous Reuters discovery material described earlier investigations as finding no evidence of wrongdoing by Joe Biden himself.\n- **Locator:** Reuters article search-result snippet only; direct fetch blocked.\n- **Source type/confidence:** reputable wire; medium-high, but **underlying source unavailable**.\n- **Period/attribution:** direct 2023 scrutiny of Biden; allegations concerned earlier vice-presidential conduct and family dealings.\n- **Contrary/mitigating:** Inquiry authorization is not a finding. Republican claims and White House denials require underlying records and final investigative outcomes.\n- **Relevant lenses:** 7B.3–7B.7, 7B.9–7B.10.\n- **Defensibility note:** Context only until the full article or congressional record is opened.\n\n### WEB-019 — Classified-documents special-counsel inquiry\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.justice.gov/storage/report-from-special-counsel-robert-k-hur-february-2024.pdf|report-on-unauthorized-removal-retention-disclosure|classified-documents-investigation`\n- **Disposition:** context\n- **Title:** “Report on the Investigation Into Unauthorized Removal, Retention, and Disclosure of Classified Documents…”\n- **Publisher/date:** US Department of Justice, Special Counsel Robert Hur, 2024-02-05\n- **Claim:** A special-counsel investigation active during 2023 examined Biden’s retention and disclosure of classified material; the 2024 report declined prosecution while making adverse factual assessments.\n- **Locator:** Exact report pages were not extractable in this run; raw PDF only.\n- **Source type/confidence:** official investigative record; high when exact findings and pages are retrieved.\n- **Period/attribution:** investigation was target-year; underlying conduct largely pre-presidential and the report post-period.\n- **Contrary/mitigating:** Must distinguish the report’s evidentiary findings, prosecutorial judgment, Biden’s cooperation, and later disputes over characterization. No exact quotation should be used without page extraction.\n- **Relevant lenses:** 7B.1–7B.2, 7B.6–7B.8, 7B.10.\n- **Defensibility note:** Not locator-qualified here.\n\n### WEB-020 — Oversight of infrastructure and climate-program implementation\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-23-106726|search-result-summary|oversight-large-program-funding`\n- **Disposition:** discovery_only\n- **Title:** “Oversight of Agency Spending: Implementing Infrastructure, CHIPS, and Climate Legislation”\n- **Publisher/date:** GAO, 2023-03-29\n- **Claim:** GAO identified major implementation and oversight questions concerning billions appropriated to Commerce, Energy, and EPA.\n- **Locator:** Search-result summary only; GAO fetch blocked.\n- **Source type/confidence:** official audit institution; potentially high after retrieval.\n- **Relevant lenses:** 5B.6–5B.7, 5B.10; 8B.2–8B.8.\n- **Defensibility note:** Discovery only; no precise findings were accessible.\n\n---\n\n# 3. Chapter-by-chapter handoff\n\n## 1B — Nuclear and existential-risk responsibility\n\n### Local baseline\n\nNo local structured 1B facts were supplied. This is an explicit evidence gap, not evidence of low exposure or responsible conduct. Biden had material nuclear authority as US president and substantial crisis exposure through Russia’s war against Ukraine, strategic competition with China, North Korea, Iran, and emerging military technologies.\n\n### Retained evidence\n\n- **WEB-001:** Offered unconditional strategic dialogue with China; post-2026 framework talks with Russia; continued adherence to New START central limits if Russia reciprocated; proposed launch notifications, crisis channels, transparency, and human-in-the-loop nuclear safeguards.\n- **WEB-002:** Proposed emerging-technology guardrails covering AI, space, cyber, and hypersonic systems; maintained a direct-ascent ASAT testing moratorium.\n\n### Lens mapping\n\n- Strongest: 1B.1, 1B.3, 1B.4, 1B.8, 1B.9.\n- Partial: 1B.6–1B.7 through multilateral/P5 engagement and expert-led policy.\n- Weak: 1B.2, because a systematic review of Biden’s own nuclear rhetoric was not completed.\n- Unresolved: 1B.5 and 1B.10. No independent assessment was retrieved of whether US nuclear posture shielded conventional coercion or whether end-2023 posture was safer overall.\n\n### Contrary evidence and gaps\n\nThe administration’s policy combined risk-reduction diplomacy with nuclear-force modernization and continued deterrence commitments. The opened speech itself acknowledges eroding arms control and adversary buildup. It does not establish that talks produced agreements or that arsenal, launch posture, command risks, or global danger declined.\n\n### Yield and diversity\n\n- Defensible source-claim units: 2.\n- Organizations: Arms Control Association/White House.\n- Source types: official policy statement reproduced by specialist NGO.\n- Below target. Needed: independent FAS/SIPRI technical assessment, force/posture changes, command-and-control review, Ukraine-crisis nuclear messaging, and end-of-year posture analysis.\n\n## 2B — International peace, restraint, and lawful security\n\n### Local baseline\n\nLF001–LF004 show very high military expenditure and substantial fiscal/GDP shares but do not identify whether spending met defensive needs, enabled aggression, or enriched networks.\n\n### Retained evidence\n\n- **WEB-003:** US vetoed a Security Council humanitarian-pauses resolution in October 2023.\n- **WEB-004:** Administration simultaneously emphasized civilian protection and Biden’s direct diplomacy.\n- Reuters discovery located additional 2023 reporting that Biden urged civilian protection and aid flows and publicly linked support for Ukraine and Israel, but direct fetch was blocked; these remain unretained discovery candidates.\n\n### Lens mapping\n\n- 2B.1 and 2B.7: Direct diplomacy is documented, but the veto supplies important contrary evidence.\n- 2B.4–2B.5: Civilian-protection rhetoric and arms-recipient responsibility require outcome evidence; the veto is materially relevant.\n- 2B.6: The public rationale—self-defense and direct diplomacy—is documented; truthfulness was not independently tested.\n- 2B.8: LP-001 supplies only spending context.\n- 2B.10: Mixed end-state evidence; Ukraine remained at war and Gaza conflict intensified.\n\n### Attribution cautions\n\nUS UN votes and presidential diplomacy are strongly attributable to the administration. Civilian harm by Ukraine, Israel, or other recipients is not automatically Biden’s conduct; evidence is needed on foreseeability, weapons transfers, conditions, leverage, and remedial action.\n\n### Yield and diversity\n\n- Defensible internet units: 2.\n- Local context units: 1.\n- Organizations: UN, SIPRI.\n- Source types: intergovernmental record, structured dataset.\n- Below target. Major missing themes: Ukraine escalation restraint and peace alternatives; weapons-transfer controls; Israeli and Ukrainian civilian-harm findings; proxy/recipient restraint; accountability for failures; Afghanistan aftermath was outside the target year except where continuing.\n\n## 3B — Domestic safety, restraint, and protection\n\n### Local baseline\n\nLF005–LF010 provide mixed country-level physical-integrity and repression observations but no incident, federal/subnational split, or Biden attribution.\n\n### Retained evidence\n\n- **WEB-005:** ICE detention population and private-facility reliance rose substantially by July 2023.\n- **WEB-006:** Continued private immigration detention diverged from Biden’s stated promise.\n- **WEB-007:** HRW documented a mixed national record, including increased incarceration and federal positive measures.\n- **WEB-008:** Federal asylum-deterrence policy allegedly exposed migrants to more dangerous routes.\n- **WEB-009:** DOJ election-threat investigations and convictions supplied a protective federal response amid continuing intimidation.\n- **WEB-011:** DOJ defended reproductive-health access against judicial restrictions.\n\n### Lens mapping\n\n- 3B.1–3B.2: Detention and asylum evidence concerns potential state-caused harm and agency accountability.\n- 3B.4–3B.5: The ICE-policy implementation gap and legal/administrative structures are directly relevant.\n- 3B.6–3B.7: Migrant, racial, reproductive, and other vulnerable-group conditions are documented.\n- 3B.8–3B.9: Election-worker protection is relevant; protest handling remains under-researched.\n- 3B.10: Evidence is mixed and attribution-limited.\n\n### Contrary evidence\n\nFederal protections and remedies included election-threat prosecutions and DOJ litigation defending medication abortion. These coexist with expanding immigration detention and asylum restrictions. Much police violence, incarceration, abortion restriction, and LGBT policy occurred under state/local rather than presidential control.\n\n### Yield and diversity\n\n- Defensible internet units: 6.\n- Local context unit: 1.\n- Organizations: ACLU, HRW, DOJ/CMS-linked records.\n- Source types: NGOs, linked administrative/legal records, structured datasets.\n- Reasonably useful but not saturated. Missing: 2023 federal police-reform implementation, protest incidents, Guantánamo, federal prison conditions, surveillance, hate-crime response, and independent remedy evidence.\n\n## 4B — Political freedom versus authoritarian rule\n\n### Local baseline\n\nLF011–LF025 show strong democratic contestability, judicial and legislative constraints, broad suffrage and civil liberties, and a middling press-freedom rank. These are inherited country conditions, not sufficient evidence of Biden’s self-binding.\n\n### Retained evidence\n\n- **WEB-009:** Federal action against threats to election officials.\n- **WEB-011:** DOJ used ordinary judicial channels to challenge abortion-access restrictions.\n- **WEB-012:** Preliminary-injunction litigation raised serious concerns about executive contacts with social-media platforms, though exact opinion pages were unavailable.\n- **WEB-007:** HRW noted federal spyware restrictions and continuing threats to democracy.\n\n### Lens mapping\n\n- 4B.1: Election administration was protected through DOJ task-force action.\n- 4B.2 and 4B.4: Robust constraints are locally documented; no evidence retrieved that Biden sought to capture courts or election machinery.\n- 4B.3, 4B.6, 4B.9: Social-media contacts supply contrary/contested evidence concerning information controls.\n- 4B.7: DOJ reproductive-rights litigation and federal equity measures provide inclusion-related evidence.\n- 4B.8: No transfer or term-limit stress occurred in 2023.\n- 4B.10: Country indicators suggest continued high freedom, but ruler-attributed strengthening remains only partly evidenced.\n\n### Important caution\n\nThe 2023 Fifth Circuit opinion was a preliminary-stage decision and later litigation changed the legal posture. It should not be described as a final adjudication that Biden personally violated the First Amendment. Exact pages and named-official findings are required.\n\n### Yield and diversity\n\n- Locator-qualified internet units: 3.\n- Context/locator-missing: 1.\n- Local context units: 3.\n- Organizations: HRW, DOJ/AP-linked reporting, Fifth Circuit, V-Dem, Freedom House, RSF.\n- Source types: structured datasets, NGO, judicial record.\n- Needed: Biden’s treatment of press access and criticism, appointments and judicial independence, executive privilege, surveillance policy, and direct evidence of respect for opposition and constitutional constraints.\n\n## 5B — Economic well-being and prosperity\n\n### Local baseline\n\nLF026–LF028 establish a wealthy, very large economy in 2023 but cannot set score direction or establish Biden’s contribution.\n\n### Retained evidence\n\n- **WEB-010:** Direct 2023 environmental-justice executive direction and institutional coordination.\n- **WEB-013:** Child-poverty reversal following expiration of expanded tax credits established an adverse inherited/continuing distributional context.\n- **WEB-014:** Medicaid unwinding caused major enrollment losses during 2023.\n- **WEB-015:** CMS corrected erroneous disenrollments and pressed states to improve procedures.\n- **WEB-016:** Biden proposed restoring the Child Tax Credit but did not secure implementation.\n- **WEB-017:** CBO discovery reported faster 2023 growth and slowing inflation, alongside a large fiscal deficit; locator retrieval remains needed.\n- **WEB-020:** GAO oversight report is a high-value implementation candidate but was not retrievable.\n\n### Lens mapping\n\n- 5B.1: Industrial/environmental strategy and CTC proposals reveal broad-based intent, but implementation varies.\n- 5B.3: Growth/inflation and deficit context is relevant, with Federal Reserve and external-cause cautions.\n- 5B.6: Environmental justice, infrastructure, climate and industrial implementation are material.\n- 5B.7: CMS’s corrective action supplies evidence of adaptation.\n- 5B.8: Child poverty, Medicaid losses and equity policy show a mixed distributional record.\n- 5B.9: The administration operated during inflation normalization and Medicaid unwinding, but causal evaluation remains incomplete.\n- 5B.10: Aggregate output was strong; household/distribution evidence is mixed.\n\n### Yield and diversity\n\n- Locator-qualified internet units: 5.\n- Context/locator-missing: 2.\n- Local context: 1.\n- Organizations: HRW, KFF, Census/CMS links, CBO, GAO, World Bank.\n- Source types: structured dataset, policy research, NGO, official analytics/audit.\n- Missing: 2023 employment and real-wage tables, independent inflation-policy causation, deficit/debt analysis, IRA/CHIPS/IIJA award and construction milestones, antitrust and competition record, strike/labor policy, regional distribution, and implementation delays.\n\n## 6B — Social well-being and human development\n\n### Local baseline\n\nNo local 6B facts were supplied. This is a gap, not evidence of unchanged welfare.\n\n### Retained evidence\n\n- **WEB-010:** Environmental-justice integration across agencies.\n- **WEB-011:** DOJ defense of medication-abortion access.\n- **WEB-013:** Child poverty more than doubled after temporary protections expired.\n- **WEB-014:** Large child Medicaid/CHIP enrollment losses occurred during 2023 unwinding.\n- **WEB-015:** CMS restored erroneous terminations and issued state guidance.\n- **WEB-016:** Biden sought renewed CTC support but lacked enactment.\n- **WEB-007/008:** Structural racial inequality and asylum-related vulnerability remained severe.\n\n### Lens mapping\n\n- 6B.1–6B.3: Welfare and equity were explicit policy priorities, while child poverty and migrant vulnerability supply major contrary outcomes.\n- 6B.4–6B.5: CMS monitoring, corrections, and guidance demonstrate some professional administration and measurement.\n- 6B.6: Medicaid unwinding was a post-pandemic transition challenge; broader disaster and crisis response was not researched sufficiently.\n- 6B.7: No direct evidence of benefits being conditioned on political loyalty; immigration detention contracting is relevant but not classic welfare clientelism.\n- 6B.8: Reproductive access, racial disparities, migrants, and environmental justice are directly pertinent.\n- 6B.9: Continuous eligibility and administrative corrections are institution-building candidates.\n- 6B.10: Mixed life-chance trajectory; high inherited capacity alone cannot support ruler credit.\n\n### Yield and diversity\n\n- Defensible units: 7.\n- Organizations: KFF, HRW, CMS/HHS/Census-linked sources.\n- Source types: policy research, NGO, administrative/legal records.\n- Missing: 2023 ACA enrollment, prescription-drug implementation, education recovery, housing and homelessness, SNAP/food insecurity, water/sanitation, veterans, disability policy, disaster response, and independent service-quality evaluations.\n\n## 7B — Personal integrity and honesty\n\n### Local baseline\n\nLF011, LF024, and LF029–LF032 show relatively strong rule-of-law/accountability and comparatively low measured corruption. They are **not** evidence of Biden’s personal honesty.\n\n### Retained evidence\n\n- **WEB-018:** Formal House impeachment inquiry concerning family business allegations; contemporaneous reporting said no personal wrongdoing finding had yet emerged. Underlying Reuters text was inaccessible.\n- **WEB-019:** Special-counsel classified-documents investigation provides a direct integrity/accountability source but requires exact page extraction and careful pre-period/target-year separation.\n- **WEB-006:** Private-detention policy diverged from a campaign promise; relevant to promise-keeping but not necessarily deliberate deception.\n- **LP-007/LP-004:** Institutional context only.\n\n### Lens mapping\n\n- 7B.1–7B.2: Classified-document statements and corrections require transcript-level investigation; current evidence is insufficiently precise.\n- 7B.3–7B.4: Family-business allegations were subject to investigation, but no accepted source here establishes Joe Biden’s personal financial benefit or official quid pro quo.\n- 7B.6: Biden’s conduct under the special-counsel and congressional investigations is a key unresolved theme.\n- 7B.7: No accepted evidence here establishes that Biden used state power to obstruct either inquiry.\n- 7B.8: Immigration-detention promise versus practice is a documented policy commitment gap, but intent and institutional constraints matter.\n- 7B.9–7B.10: Appointments, nepotism, ethics waivers, gifts, and public-trust effects remain under-researched.\n\n### Strongest contrary evidence discipline\n\n- An inquiry is not a finding.\n- Hunter Biden’s conduct is not automatically Joe Biden’s.\n- The classified-document matter involved conduct largely before the 2023 presidency; only investigation response and target-year statements are directly in period.\n- Country corruption indicators cannot substitute for ruler-specific integrity evidence.\n\n### Yield and diversity\n\n- Locator-qualified internet units: 1 cross-chapter promise item.\n- Context/underlying-source-missing: 2.\n- Local context: 2 groups.\n- Not saturated and below evidentiary target. Required continuation: exact Hur-report pages, Biden interview/press transcripts, White House cooperation timeline, House bank-record allegations and responses, ethics disclosures, gifts, appointments, and adverse/clearing findings from independent sources.\n\n## 8B — Effectiveness and competence\n\n### Frozen 2023 program portfolio suggested by evidence\n\nA balanced portfolio should include more than survival or one favored policy:\n\n1. Implement IRA, CHIPS, and infrastructure/industrial policy.\n2. Continue economic recovery while reducing inflation.\n3. Support Ukraine and manage strategic competition without direct great-power war.\n4. Manage Israel–Gaza crisis and humanitarian consequences.\n5. Protect democratic administration and voting processes.\n6. Administer post-pandemic social-policy transitions, including Medicaid unwinding.\n7. Advance equity/environmental-justice goals.\n8. Manage immigration and asylum.\n\nMoral approval or disapproval of these objectives must remain outside 8B.\n\n### Retained evidence\n\n- **WEB-001/002:** Clear nuclear-risk strategy, specific diplomatic proposals, and institutional guardrails.\n- **WEB-003/004:** Direct diplomacy and a disputed UN strategy in Gaza.\n- **WEB-005/006:** Immigration detention shows implementation capability but movement away from a stated campaign commitment.\n- **WEB-009:** DOJ task-force implementation against election threats.\n- **WEB-010:** Executive-order operationalization of environmental justice.\n- **WEB-014/015:** Medicaid unwinding exposed major administrative harm, followed by a measurable federal correction.\n- **WEB-017:** Macroeconomic outcomes improved, but attribution and exact locator remain incomplete.\n- **WEB-020:** GAO report is a critical unresolved implementation source for major spending laws.\n\n### Lens mapping\n\n- 8B.1–8B.2: Multiple explicit programs and formal mechanisms are documented.\n- 8B.3–8B.5: DOJ, CMS, and whole-of-government environmental-justice actions show mobilization; immigration and Medicaid failures expose coordination weaknesses.\n- 8B.6: Delivery is evident in enforcement/correction and executive direction, but major industrial-policy outcome evidence is missing.\n- 8B.7: Aggregate economic movement and social-policy setbacks are mixed and causally contested.\n- 8B.8: CMS reinstatement and guidance are direct corrective-action evidence.\n- 8B.9: Ukraine/Gaza diplomacy and election-threat response are relevant crisis-management evidence.\n- 8B.10: End-of-year progress varies sharply by portfolio component; durable infrastructure/industrial results need independent data.\n\n### Yield and diversity\n\n- Defensible cross-mapped internet units: 9.\n- Context/discovery candidates: 3.\n- Organizations: ACA/White House, UN, ACLU, HRW, KFF/CMS, CBO, GAO.\n- Source types: official policy, intergovernmental proceeding, NGOs, policy research, administrative/audit analytics.\n- Near normal quantity but not saturated because the central IRA/CHIPS/IIJA implementation chain remains weakly documented.\n\n---\n\n# 4. Discovery and rejected-candidate accounting\n\n## Query provenance\n\nThe following themes were searched through MiniMax Search after Brave quota failure:\n\n- `Joe Biden 2023 nuclear arms control risk reduction Russia China FAS SIPRI`\n- `Joe Biden 2023 Ukraine Gaza diplomacy civilian harm arms transfer UN Reuters`\n- `Biden administration 2023 police immigration detention human rights protest report`\n- `Joe Biden 2023 political freedom press courts democracy opposition Reuters`\n- `Biden 2023 economy inflation jobs infrastructure implementation CBO GAO`\n- `Biden 2023 health education poverty social policy implementation`\n- `Joe Biden 2023 integrity ethics classified documents Hunter investigation findings`\n- `Biden administration effectiveness 2023 IRA CHIPS infrastructure implementation GAO`\n- Named-source follow-ups for Arms Control Association, State Department, UN, HRW, Fifth Circuit, CBO, KFF, Reuters impeachment reporting, and GAO implementation oversight.\n\n## Rejected or limited candidates\n\n- Facebook reposts: rejected as discovery-only and unnecessary where underlying sources existed.\n- YouTube Reuters clips: rejected because video snippets lacked precise transcript locators.\n- Wikipedia and Ballotpedia: context/discovery only; not needed as final evidence.\n- Partisan House committee releases and party memoranda: not accepted as sole evidence for contested integrity allegations.\n- Official White House fact sheets: generally not used as sole implementation evidence.\n- Raw PDFs without extracted page text: kept as context/discovery, not locator-qualified.\n- Reuters pages blocked by robots: snippets retained only as candidate context.\n- Post-2023 sources: used only where they directly described or investigated 2023 events; otherwise excluded.\n\n---\n\n# 5. Cross-chapter reuse and source concentration\n\n- **WEB-005/006** legitimately cross-map to 3B, 6B, 7B, and 8B because they establish one federal detention-system fact and one promise-to-implementation gap. They must not be duplicated under new IDs.\n- **WEB-007–011** are same-URL splits from the HRW country chapter. They represent materially distinct claims with separate HTML sections/paragraphs. Source concentration should be reported: several chapters depend heavily on one HRW report.\n- **WEB-013–016** are same-URL splits from KFF but address separate claims: poverty baseline, Medicaid losses, federal correction, and CTC proposal. Again, source concentration is material.\n- **WEB-001/002** are separate materially distinct claims from one arms-control speech: nuclear diplomacy/safeguards and emerging-technology guardrails.\n- Local facts LP-003–LP-005 overlap in chapter routing but remain separate because they measure democratic freedoms, institutional constraints, and press freedom.\n\n---\n\n# 6. Self-audit\n\n## Temporal fit\n\n- Strong direct 2023 fit: WEB-001–WEB-012, WEB-014–WEB-016.\n- Retrospective but directly about 2023: WEB-013–WEB-016 and WEB-017.\n- Predominantly pre-period conduct investigated in/post 2023: WEB-019; use cautiously.\n- Continuing commitments originating before 2023: WEB-006 and Child Tax Credit history; target-year relevance is the continued implementation outcome.\n\n## Ruler attribution\n\nStrongest attribution:\n\n- Presidential-administration nuclear strategy.\n- US Security Council votes and Biden’s direct diplomacy.\n- Executive orders.\n- DOJ/CMS federal actions.\n\nWeaker/shared attribution:\n\n- Aggregate economic outcomes.\n- National incarceration and rights conditions.\n- State abortion restrictions.\n- Medicaid unwinding administered by states under federal rules.\n- ICE levels influenced by migration flows and congressional appropriations.\n- Family-business allegations without proof of Biden’s direction or benefit.\n\n## Context-only items\n\nAll local priors are country/state context rather than direct personal conduct. WEB-012, WEB-017, WEB-018, WEB-019, and WEB-020 lack sufficient underlying locator extraction in this run and should not be counted as final defensible evidence yet.\n\n## Claims needing independent corroboration\n\n- Effectiveness of nuclear-risk proposals.\n- Actual civilian-protection consequences of US Gaza diplomacy and arms support.\n- Conditions and abuse patterns inside ICE facilities beyond detention counts.\n- First Amendment merits and individual Biden attribution in the social-media litigation.\n- Causal contribution of Biden policy to 2023 growth, inflation, employment, and deficit.\n- Any allegation involving Biden’s family business or classified materials.\n- Implementation outcomes of IRA, CHIPS, and IIJA.\n\n---\n\n# 7. Local-data audit\n\n## Retained as context\n\n- **LF001–LF004:** Military expenditure.\n- **LF005–LF010:** Repression and physical-integrity baseline.\n- **LF011–LF025:** Accountability, democratic freedom, institutional constraints, press freedom, rule of law, suffrage.\n- **LF026–LF028:** GDP, GDP per capita, population.\n- **LF029–LF032:** Corruption context.\n\nThus, all 32 supplied facts were retained as context. None was promoted to direct ruler-conduct evidence.\n\n## Used most substantively\n\n- 2B: LF001–LF004.\n- 3B: LF005–LF010.\n- 4B: LF011–LF025.\n- 5B: LF026–LF028.\n- 7B: LF011, LF024, LF029–LF032.\n- 8B: LF011 and LF024.\n\n## Not directly useful for score-bearing attribution\n\nNo supplied local fact directly establishes a Biden choice, order, correction, conflict of interest, program implementation, or personal outcome. Candidate methodology mappings were therefore treated as routing hints only.\n\n## Empty local chapters\n\n- 1B: no supplied local fact.\n- 6B: no supplied local fact.\n\nThese remain explicit gaps and were not converted into “not applicable,” peaceful, safe, or zero conditions.\n\n---\n\n# 8. Continuation priorities\n\nThe highest-value continuation would retrieve at least two genuinely new units for every under-covered chapter:\n\n1. **1B:** Independent FAS/SIPRI evaluation of 2023 US arsenal/posture and arms-control outcome.\n2. **2B:** UN/ICRC/CRS material on Ukraine and Gaza arms transfers, civilian harm, conditions, and peace alternatives.\n3. **3B:** DOJ pattern-or-practice policing record, federal prison/Guantánamo evidence, and protest treatment.\n4. **4B:** Exact Fifth Circuit pages plus Supreme Court procedural history; press-access and executive-constraint evidence.\n5. **5B:** BEA/BLS tables, CBO fiscal analysis, and independent IRA/CHIPS/IIJA implementation evaluations.\n6. **6B:** CMS ACA enrollment, prescription-drug rollout, food insecurity, housing/homelessness, and education recovery.\n7. **7B:** Exact Hur-report pages; congressional bank-record evidence and responses; ethics disclosures and appointment/conflict records.\n8. **8B:** GAO implementation findings and milestone/outcome evidence across the frozen portfolio.\n\nThis research supports a usable but uneven eight-chapter dossier. Chapters 3B, 6B, and 8B have the strongest narrative yield; 1B, 2B, and especially 7B require targeted continuation before they are reasonably saturated."}}
